# Art Deco Marquise Engagement Ring ({{year}}): 4 Details That Signal Real

![Three loose marquise cut diamonds held in jeweller tweezers against a soft grey backdrop as the DiamondCritics featured image for the {{year}} art deco marquise engagement ring guide titled 4 details that signal real](/images/art-deco-marquise-engagement-ring.jpg "Art Deco Marquise Engagement Ring 4 Details That Signal Real")

## TL;DR — Art Deco Marquise Engagement Ring in One Scroll

**An art deco marquise engagement ring borrows design language from the 1920s–1930s — geometric lines, platinum metalwork, milgrain beading, baguette or trapezoid side stones, and bold architectural shoulders.** True period Art Deco rings from the 1920s are rare and often $10,000+. Modern Art Deco-inspired settings from certified retailers deliver the exact same visual language with GIA-cert stones from $1,275. Knowing the 4 signature details lets you spot authentic Art Deco design — whether buying a period piece or a modern reproduction.

**The 4 details that signal real Art Deco (all 4 covered in full below):**

- **Detail 1 — Milgrain beading on prongs and shank.** 1920s jewellers used milgrain (beaded edge detail) as a signature finish. Real Art Deco rings have crisp, uniform milgrain. Modern knock-offs skip it or apply it poorly.
- **Detail 2 — Baguette or trapezoid side stones.** Period Art Deco favoured step-cut geometric accents — baguettes, trapezoids, half-moons. Round brilliants as side stones = post-1950 modern, not Art Deco.
- **Detail 3 — Platinum metal (not yellow gold).** Platinum became engagement ring standard in 1912 and dominated 1920s Art Deco. Yellow gold Art Deco pieces exist but are rarer. Rose gold Art Deco is almost non-existent in period pieces.
- **Detail 4 — Geometric architectural shoulders.** Art Deco shoulders have straight lines, stepped tiers, hexagonal frames, or chevron cuts. Soft curves and floral motifs = Edwardian or Victorian, not Art Deco.

